<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#carrack">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carrack</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A carrack was a large, three- or four-masted ocean-going sailing ship developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in the fifteenth century. Its size allowed it to carry large cargoes and defend itself on long voyages. Carracks were the mainstay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>'s long-distance trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carrack.jpg" alt="Carrack" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portuguese_Carracks_off_a_Rocky_Coast.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
